ng. They were out of food. We didn't-- we didn't have anything to eat or drink. And a partisan saw a little house down the mountain. He went. There was a farmhouse. So he got a piece of-- he came back with a piece of pork like this thick, white, just white. I don't know what you call that. White? No, there's no meat on it, just the fat. This thick and this big. And he had a little knife, sharp knife, and he was cutting slices. And every person got 1 inch, like a square inch of this pork. And when they gave to my mom, she said, "I'll die first. I won't put this in my mouth." You know, Jewish people don't eat pork. And then I said, "I won't eat mine either." "Oh," she said, "yes, you will. You eat that, and you're going to eat my portion." So she made me eat two portions of pork because she said, it'll give you strength, you know? So I ate that. And my sister ate her portion. But anyhow, we walked and walked on for months. Maybe we were walking three, four months. And then finally-- oh, yeah, one time It was a terrible thing that happened. My mom, she had no strength. She was a wonderful walker, because she walked to the business four times a day. You know, she would go from our house to center city and back. And then again, 1 o'clock they would fiesta. They closed the store till 4 o'clock. She would-- she would go back and forth four times. She was a wonderful walker. But this was too much for her, walking in the mountains with partisans. It was a terrible, terrible ordeal. And she became so worn out and washed out and depressed. And she had no strength. She just fell. One night, we were walking, and she just fell down. And she said, "Just let me die. Promise me you're going to save yourself and your sister." And I begged my mom, "Please, please, please try a little bit. Try. You know, get some strength. Try pick yourself up." "No, I'm done. I just want to die." And she just-- it was-- that was the worst day of my life. And she kept insisting, you go and save yourself and save your sister and let me die. And she wouldn't budge. And the partisans, they don't have time for a woman there, you know? They just kept going. And you had to follow them because, you know, time was very urgent because before dawn, you have to stop and hide. As soon as it gets light, we all have to find hiding places and hide. So we left, and I was so upset. And I was praying for my mom. And then the next day, after we walked that night, and we were hiding the whole day, and the next night when we started walking again, a partisan comes with my mom. Oh, my god. I was so happy. Oh, my god. And that was the happiest day of my life. Oh, my god. I couldn't believe it. And she got a little strength, and he helped her. And he was holding on to her. And he brought her back. And so that was a lucky thing. And we walked and walked like every-- always the same thing. At night, we walk. During the day, we hide. And finally, we came to the end of the mountains, and there was no food, no ammunition. Partisans had nothing. And they started signaling to the British and Americans across the Adriatic in Italy. We are neighbors. Italy and Croatia is across the Adriatic Sea. So they were signaling to them. And they told them that they have women and children, and they are-- everybody's starving, and there is no ammunition. They have no ammunition. They have nothing. So the Americans signaled back, "We're going to help you. We're going to send you ammunition, and we're going to send you food." The next day, we have parachutes fall-- coming down. A lot of food and ammunition and everything. So that was a lucky thing. And then we were there for a couple of weeks. The next best thing that they signaled, the Americans, we're going to send a ship for you. And we're going to take the women and children and bring them here For safety. So we were going-- do you know, it was such an ordeal going down those mountains to come to the bottom, to the water. Would take three hours every night. And then we had to go back, climb up on our hands and feet, you know, climb up to hide during the day. The ship didn't come. We went like that, up and down, about six nights. And the ship didn't show up. And then, finally, one night we come down. And there's a ship in the middle, a battleship in the middle of the ocean-- not ocean, the Adriatic Sea. And now the point is they cannot come to the shore. It's a big battleship there in the middle of the Adriatic Sea there like-- and the partisans, they had hidden a rowboat behind the rocks, big rocks, a rowboat that could hold maybe like 15 people. And we were a big group of people there. And who's going to get in there first? So my mom said to the leader, the one that was with the rifle, if you could put me and my two kids in this rowboat first, I'll give you everything I have. And he agreed. I don't know what my mom gave him. I don't know what she had, money, jewelry. I don't know what she had. But my mom said, take off your sweater. Take off your shoes. My sister, everybody, give them. Give him. Give him. We gave him everything we had on. I gave him my knapsack with the blanket, with everything. And I don't know what else my mom gave him. Look, I was a child, you know, I didn't ask. But I heard my mom said to him, I'll give you everything I have. So I don't know what she had, but he put us on the boat first. And then the rest of the people, not everybody could go. Only so many people went. And the partisans were rowing very fast to the battleship. And there were some shooting at us. The Germans saw that there was some movement there. And-- but they didn't-- they didn't get us. And we came to the ship, and the British-- it was a British battleship. And they threw down the ropes, and we climbed up. They helped us. We had to climb up the ropes because we were on a little rowboat, and the battleship was so tall, high. And so when they-- when they pulled me up, I was-- you know, I was strong. I was holding good. When they pulled me up, and I put my feet on that battleship, I said, thank you, dear God, I am saved. You know? [LAUGHING] And then my sister, they pulled my sister. And, well, the partisans were helping, you know, the ones that couldn't do it. And the soldiers from the ship and the partisans were pulling them up, and the other ones were pulling them up. And everybody made it. And they were very nice. They wanted to give us all kinds of food right away, candy, chocolate, bananas. My mom said to me, don't take anything because your stomach shrunk. You might get sick. So I only accepted one banana, I remember. And they took us to Italy. And when we came to Italy, they took us to a camp, a dispersed people's camp. And the first day, of course, we didn't see any people. They took us in a big room. They fumigated our clothes. They washed our clothes. They were shaving. Oh, my god. Everybody, the women got their heads shaved like boys. And I don't know what. Me and my sister were so lucky. They let us go. They didn't shave our hair. I'll never forget that. I was so happy. Because I was so scared. Oh, my god, they're going to shave me. You know, because people had lice and all kind of-- look, we were in our clothes for maybe a year in the same dress, in the same clothes, no water for drinking, let alone to take a shower or something. And it was terrible. So what they did-- but they washed our clothes, and they gave it back to us. And so the next day, we went walking outside. And somebody recognized my mom and told her that they saw her brother in the camp there. And then we started asking the authority, you know, there where he is. And we found him. And then he tells us that my father and my brother are in the next town. That's called Lecce. We were in Bari, Bari, Italy. And this little town was called Lecce. It was like an hour away. And we were so happy. And then the authorities, they notified my father. And he came with my brother. And we were united. And that was so wonderful. And the reason my father was in Lecce-- because the-- my father worked for the American Census Bureau. He knew languages, and they needed people to translate and to read certain letters or certain, you know, whatever they needed. So my father was a good person for that. And they were either paying him, I don't know what. If he was volunteering or they were paying, I'm not sure. But anyhow, so he was doing that. And so my father said to us, we were so happy we were united, everybody except my older sister, Blanka. And my father said, what a lucky break that you happen to come now. Because in two weeks, we're going to the United States. And we were so surprised. So my father went to the authorities and said to them, my wife and my two children just came, and we're supposed to go to the United States. Can they come in the group? And they, of course, said, yes. And what happened? President Roosevelt in 1944, because he did not do anything to help the refugees or the Jewish people, or when the ship came from Germany with 1,200 Jewish people that they were trying to save themselves, President Roosevelt didn't allow them to come in. Nobody allowed them. And they had to go back to Europe and be killed by Nazis. But he decided it's almost the war is turning around. He decided that he wanted to do one little thing to show the world that he did something. So he invited 1,000 refugees to come to the United States as a haven until the end of the war. At the end of the war, before we entered the United States, we had to sign that the day the war ends, we have to go back. We had to go back. We only came as a haven. And so it was. Oh, my god, it was so much excitement. We're going to the United States, you know? And they only wanted to invite 1,000. And there were thousands of people that wanted to come, refugees. But 1,000, that's all. And they didn't even bring 1,000. President Roosevelt said 1,000, and they only brought 890 or something like that. I don't know why. Because they were investigating every person. They didn't want to just bring anybody. And so we came to the United States. We boarded a ship, I think, in Naples. And ship, a convoy of ships. We were like 14 ships, three destroyers in the front, three destroyers in the back. [PAUSES] There were army ship. There was a ship full of German soldiers, the ones that they caught. You know, what do you call them?

INT: Prisoners of war.

SM: Prisoners of war. One big ship full of prisoners of war.

INT: Mhm.

SM: One ship of American wounded soldiers. This other ship that we were on, a big ship, we were a half a ship, 1,000 people in a half a ship. And the other half was another group of American wounded soldiers. And there were other ships. And We were going in a rotation like this, three ships, then more ships, then in the back the battleships. And it was wartime, you know, 1944. This was 1944. And it was very dangerous. And it was a hard, hard trip because we had German planes over us. They had to put smokes, you know, smoke-- what do you call it?

INT: Smoke screen?

SM: Screen, yeah. Smoke screen. And [PAUSES] we had to go downstairs under. And everybody was choking and fainting. It was-- the smoke was terrible. And that was one thing. Another thing that was-- the ship stopped still because they thought that there was a-- what do you call them?

