SM: Mines and stuff like that. And they didn't go straight like normal. They went around and around. It took us a long time to come to the United States. I think about three weeks or more. And anyhow, well, I was excited on the ship. I was-- I didn't care, you know, nothing. I was a kid, and everything was great for me. I was happy. I was helping other people. There were people that couldn't get up, couldn't eat. And I was like doing mitzvot, you know? I was getting them food. I was-- there were a couple of ladies. They had one blouse, and it was all messed up because people-- was rough ocean, and people were throwing up. And they had no strength. It was hard because we had those bunks, you know, one on top of the other. It wasn't luxury travel. It was a very hard travel. And so I was helping these people. And I remember this lady, she blessed me. She took off her blouse, and I washed it for her. And I hung it up, and then I gave it to her when it dried up. She had a clean blouse to wear. Because the food, everything was going all over her. And she couldn't get up. She was just laying on that bunk. Was one bunk on top of-- see, it was like an army ship, you know? And it was-- and so after all that, we came to-- oh, on the ship, you know, people-- it was a nice group of people, these people. Mostly Jewish people, but there was like about 50 non-Jewish people. And they were performers. They decided they wanted to make a show. And they made a show, and they show to the wounded soldiers. They entertained them. And they were-- there was an opera singer. One guy was an opera singer. There was a pianist. There were a couple of pianists. There were different people that performed. And, you know, they tried to pass time like that. And of course, there was danger. I mean, we were on the ocean, war time, you know, and everything. But look, you had to pass time, you know? And I was-- I met right away two girls, and I became very good friends with my two girls, two girlfriends there. And then when we came to the United States, and the whole time we were friends. But anyhow, then we came. We came to the United States, to New York, and we were very excited. First thing we saw was the Statue of Liberty. We all said prayers and thank God for, you know, making us come safe to United States and bringing us there and experience this wonderful country, you know? And we saw the Statue of Liberty, and it was amazing. And we were so happy. And we stayed overnight. The next night, we-- they had trains right near the ship. They had trains. We went into the trains. And they took us to Oswego, New York, to Camp Ontario. And they had-- that was an army camp. And they had barracks there. And they made barracks like according to the families. They divided the barracks. When it was army camp, it was all one. A barrack was all one space. But they divided it in sections. If you were five people, a family, they made a section for you inside the camp in the barrack. And so you had like a place to socialize. And you had a couple of bedrooms. They divided it. They put like what do you call that? Like the sheet wood. You know? What do you call it?

