om the-- director from the camp-- they had a director. And director from the camp, he was a very nice man. He went to the government, and he says, "Why should these people go back when they're here in the United States?" And [PAUSES] everything is torn there. There's nothing there. And President Truman allowed 3,000 a month to come after the war because he saw that there was such destruction and such hardship. He passed a law that every month they could bring in 3,000 refugees to the United States. So they said, why these people are coming and you're making these other people go back? So President Truman said, no, they shouldn't go back. If they-- it's up to them. If they want to stay, they could stay. Of course, everybody wanted to stay. So they arranged very nicely for us, everything. They took us to Canada. They had our first papers and all the information, everything that was needed to come legally to United States. We crossed the bridge, and we came legally to United States. And five years later, we got our citizenship, and we became American citizens.

