SM: And-- oh, yeah. After the war, they were closing the camp. And everybody had to go someplace. And so when we came back from Canada, my father wanted to go to California. He heard so much about California. He wanted to go to California. But they said to him, no, don't go to California. It's too far, and you might not get a job. Go to Philadelphia. Philadelphia is better for you, so they told us. So my father said, all right. So we came to Philadelphia. And they brought us here on a train, escorted us to Philadelphia. And the Jewish Federation met us, and they told us they found that apartment for us. And they took us to our apartment, which was empty, not a thing of furniture, nothing, empty apartment. It was January. It was cold, no heat, no hot water, nothing. It was on the third floor on 4th and Pine. And, [PAUSES] well, they did ask us, "Do you need any help?" My father was such a proud man. To him, to ask for help was, like, terrible. He said, "No, we don't need any help. We're going to get by ourselves." So my father had a few hundred dollars, nothing much to talk about. So he went on South Street there. There were old stores, old furniture stores. And so he bought few old beds, used. And he bought used couple of pots and pans and used silver, everything, a few plates, everything from a used store on South Street. And so we got by like that. And right away, we looked for a job. But we were living in a cold apartment in January because the electric company would not give us electricity or any-- oh, yeah, dark too, no light-- because we had no credit. We just came. We were refugees. We just came. Who's going to give us credit? But finally, you know, we got a neighbor, and they helped us out. He worked, you know, called up and told them, these people are refugees. They just came. And finally, we got electricity, and we got hot water and heat. And we took any kind of job we could. My mother went to work in Botany 500, clothing, suits. And she never did anything, sewing or anything. She was a business lady. But she was doing the collar. She was a finisher. And my father went to work for Stetson Hats. That-- in those days, in 19-- we came here 1951, I think. Yeah, 1951. No, no, we came-- what am I saying? 1940-- '47. We came here in 1947. People were wearing hats. Every man wore a hat. And Stetson was a big company. My father got a job. He was a-- [PAUSES] putting hats in a box. What do you call it?

